WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.560
Film Courage: What's the most important part
of the writing process for you?

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:04.310
Josh Healey, Writer/Producer/Comedian - The
North Pole Show: I don't know what the most

00:00:04.310 --> 00:00:09.740
important part of the writing process is but
I know that the the thing that I've learned

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:17.949
over the last couple years that's been the
most helpful and the most for me why I transitioned

00:00:17.949 --> 00:00:24.810
from more solo performance and solo writing
work to screenwriting and being part of a

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:32.309
writers’ room is for me writing is not an
individual process.

00:00:32.309 --> 00:00:40.390
Actually I tried to do the thing where you
sit at the desk by yourself and write a book,

00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:45.010
write the novel, write the…and that's just
not me, I couldn't do it.

00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:56.379
And so for me I write my first drafts with
people or I get ideas talking with people.

00:00:56.379 --> 00:00:58.000
Just “What do you think about this?”

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:03.000
And the writing happens usually before I'm
even writing.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.979
The writing is in conversation.

00:01:04.979 --> 00:01:08.969
The writing is trying stuff out, trying ideas…
questions.

00:01:08.969 --> 00:01:11.389
I ask a lot of questions.

00:01:11.389 --> 00:01:19.999
And then the actual writing for me is I'm
still pen and paper.

00:01:19.999 --> 00:01:22.890
I don't start on Final Draft.

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:27.729
I don't start on the computer because the
Internet hurts me.

00:01:27.729 --> 00:01:29.950
And it’s not even the Internet it’s just
the screen.

00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:36.779
There is something about the pressure of the
screen and so it takes longer because I have

00:01:36.779 --> 00:01:44.139
to freakin’ transcribe everything but I
get sooner to what I actually want.

00:01:44.139 --> 00:01:52.789
So I always start pen and paper and it allows
me to get free and really what it does is

00:01:52.789 --> 00:01:58.499
it removes the editor in my head while I’m
writing if I’m on the screen I’m editing

00:01:58.499 --> 00:01:59.749
while I write.

00:01:59.749 --> 00:02:07.390
If I’m just writing freehand I get a little
more…that’s usually where the good stuff

00:02:07.390 --> 00:02:08.899
comes out…usually.

00:02:08.899 --> 00:02:12.680
Film Courage: You do something called ‘Why
I Write.’

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:16.489
You stand up and do Open Mic Nights and different
things?

00:02:16.489 --> 00:02:24.120
Josh: Yes, so as a writer I got my start doing
performance poetry and spoken word and then

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:32.569
I did a little bit of stand up and then I
moved into live storytelling for this show

00:02:32.569 --> 00:02:37.120
on NPR called Snap Judgment.

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:44.579
So I did that for awhile and most of that
is autobiographical or semi-autobiographical

00:02:44.579 --> 00:02:54.420
or supposedly autobiographical but at a certain
point I got to…you know I have stories.

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:58.670
I can tell you stories about my life and that’s
great and I love to do that but there are

00:02:58.670 --> 00:03:02.120
other stories as a writer that I want to tell
other than my own freakin’ life!

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:08.579
And there is my whole community and my neighbors
and my neighbors and my friends and my family.

00:03:08.579 --> 00:03:12.379
I want to do more than just me on a microphone.

00:03:12.379 --> 00:03:20.340
Yvan Iturriaga and I knew each other just
from Oakland [California) and actually we

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:23.260
met playing soccer.

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:29.290
We didn’t start just working together we
were friends/homies playing in a socialist

00:03:29.290 --> 00:03:35.469
soccer club in Oakland called Left-Wing Football
Club.

00:03:35.469 --> 00:03:40.620
I don’t even remember how we got started…Oh…I
just wanted to make some videos.

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:45.049
It’s early 2010 or something.

00:03:45.049 --> 00:03:48.239
Internet videos, blogs, vlogs, all of these
things.

00:03:48.239 --> 00:03:49.370
I was like “I want to do something.

00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:53.060
You are the filmmaker in our crew, let’s
do something.”

00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:55.989
And I think some of those still exist on Youtube.

00:03:55.989 --> 00:03:58.420
They are terrible, they are freakin’ awful.

00:03:58.420 --> 00:04:01.790
I mean the cinematography is amazing but I
didn’t know what I was doing.

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:05.010
I didn’t know how to write for film but
it’s just experimenting.

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:12.540
It’s just trying stuff out and then little
from little going there to making larger videos,

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:16.891
having slightly larger budgets and then going
from there to making the web series [The North

00:04:16.891 --> 00:04:16.903
Pole Show on YouTube].

